Ladybird’s webNavigation events come from the UI process’s navigation
points, as Firefox’s WebNavigation.sys.mjs takes them from the
parent’s web progress listeners and Chromium’s WebNavigationTabObserver
from WebContentsObserver. The details carry what Firefox’s
ext-webNavigation.js fills in: url, timeStamp, frameId, parentFrameId,
tabId, and windowId; transitionType is “link” at the top and
“auto_subframe” in frames, Firefox’s defaults, with no qualifiers.
Like Firefox, it reports nothing for a navigable in no tab, a
private tab an extension may not see, an error page, or a frame’s
initial about:blank. onCreatedNavigationTarget waits for the new
view’s first navigation, since that’s when its URL is known, as in
Chromium’s WebNavigationTabObserver::DidOpenRequestedURL. WebKit’s
WebExtensionContextAPIWebNavigation has getFrame and getAllFrames
too, and the same events.
